\newlabel{sol}{{1.3}{8}{Solenoidal porcupine-like graph}{theorem.1.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  Schematic representation of the geometric dichotomy for maximal attractors in skew products with non-uniformly contracting fibers. In the absence of a continuous invariant graph, the maximal attractor exhibits a \emph  {solenoidal porcupine structure}, consisting of a measurable invariant graph (the central core) decorated by uncountably many embedded $C^{1}$ spines. Each spine arises from stable remnants of random fiber compositions and varies measurably with the base point. The figure illustrates how expansion in the base, combined with weak contraction and parabolic behavior in the fibers, destroys global graph regularity while preserving a compact attracting set, as described in Theorem \ref {thm1}. }}{9}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dichotomy}{{1}{9}{Schematic representation of the geometric dichotomy for maximal attractors in skew products with non-uniformly contracting fibers. In the absence of a continuous invariant graph, the maximal attractor exhibits a \emph {solenoidal porcupine structure}, consisting of a measurable invariant graph (the central core) decorated by uncountably many embedded $C^{1}$ spines. Each spine arises from stable remnants of random fiber compositions and varies measurably with the base point. The figure illustrates how expansion in the base, combined with weak contraction and parabolic behavior in the fibers, destroys global graph regularity while preserving a compact attracting set, as described in Theorem \ref {thm1}}{figure.1}{}}
